artifact.self_contained_problem:
The time-integrated spectra of the prompt gamma-ray emission from gamma-ray bursts (GRBs) are commonly well fit by the empirical Band function, defined as a smoothly broken power law

  N(E) = A (E/100 keV)^α exp(-E/E0),  for E ≤ (α − β) E0,
  N(E) = A [(α − β) E0/100 keV]^(α−β) exp(β − α) (E/100 keV)^β,  for E > (α − β) E0,

with low-energy photon index α (typically α ≈ −1), high-energy photon index β (typically β ≈ −2 to −2.5), and a peak energy E_peak = (2 + α) E0 in the νF_ν representation typically of order 10²–10³ keV.

Determine which physical radiation mechanism is responsible for producing this prompt spectrum. The mechanism must self-consistently account for:
 (i) the observed distribution of low-energy spectral slopes α, including events that violate the synchrotron "line of death" (α > −2/3) and the fast-cooling synchrotron limit (α > −3/2);
 (ii) the inferred electron cooling regime, given that radiative cooling timescales are generally much shorter than the dynamical timescale;
 (iii) the observed values and clustering of the peak energy E_peak.

The candidate mechanisms to be discriminated among include:
 (a) optically thin synchrotron emission from accelerated leptons in internal shocks or magnetic reconnection sites;
 (b) jitter (small-scale random magnetic field) radiation from accelerated leptons;
 (c) synchrotron self-Compton emission;
 (d) Comptonization of thermal or photospheric (quasi-blackbody) seed photons by a dissipative photosphere;
 (e) hadronic radiation processes (e.g., proton synchrotron, photo-pion induced cascades).

Identify the dominant prompt-emission radiation mechanism, or establish a definitive multi-component framework, that simultaneously satisfies (i)–(iii) for the observed GRB population.

